Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period adventure drama, naturalistic faces, fur, frost, and fabric; 1890s California-to-Yukon palette; no stylized cartoon animals.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Sun-kissed lawns, poplars, a wide cool veranda. BUCK, a massive hundred-forty-pound dog—St. Bernard bulk, Scotch shepherd shape, rich brown coat with white chest splash—bounds through grass like a king.

(SOUND: birdsong, distant fountain splash, lazy summer insects)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every tide-water dog from Puget Sound to San Diego.

Buck plunges into the cement swimming tank. JUDGE MILLER'S grandsons cling to his back. He stalks past yelping fox terriers, ignoring the house dogs Toots and Ysabel.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Men had found a yellow metal in the Northland. They wanted heavy dogs, strong of muscle, with furry coats against the frost.

INT. JUDGE MILLER'S LIBRARY - NIGHT

A roaring fire. Buck lies at JUDGE MILLER's feet, eyes half-closed, utterly secure.

(SOUND: fire crackle, clock tick, wind soft at the windows)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here Buck ruled. He was born here. He had lived the four years of his life a sated aristocrat—until the fall of eighteen ninety-seven.

EXT. ORCHARD PATH - NIGHT

MANUEL, a gardener's helper, leads Buck on what seems a stroll. Buck trusts him. They reach a little flag station: COLLEGE PARK. A STRANGER waits. Money chinks between hands.

(SOUND: gravel under boots, night insects, coins)

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles stout rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls. The rope tightens. He springs. The stranger grapples his throat, throws him, chokes him senseless.

(SOUND: rope rasp, Buck's choked snarl, body thud)

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

Buck wakes jolting. Locomotive shriek. He lunges; jaws close on the stranger's hand until choked out again.

(SOUND: train wheels clatter, whistle shriek, struggle)

STRANGER
Yep, has fits. Takin' 'm up for the boss to 'Frisco.

INT. SHED BEHIND SALOON - SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT - NIGHT

The stranger's hand is wrapped bloody. SALOON-KEEPER helps file off Buck's brass collar. Buck is flung into a cagelike crate.

(SOUND: file on brass, crate slam, waterfront foghorn distant)

SALOON-KEEPER
That makes a hundred and fifty—and he's worth it.

Buck rages through the bars. The saloon-keeper's candle-lit face peers in. Buck's joyful bark twists into a savage growl.

EXT. HIGH-WALLED BACK YARD - SEATTLE - DAY

Four men set the crate down. MAN IN RED SWEATER—stout, sweater sagging at the neck—signs for it, brings hatchet and club.

(SOUND: crate wood splinter, distant harbor gulls)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

The crate opens. Buck launches one hundred forty pounds of fury. The club checks him mid-air. Again. A dozen charges. Blood from nose and mouth. A frightful blow on the nose. Buck crumples, senseless.

(SOUND: club impacts, Buck's scream-snarl, men shouting from the wall)

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy—we've had our little ruction. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you.

He pats the pounded head. Buck endures it. Drinks. Bolts raw meat from the man's hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver—a master to be obeyed, though not necessarily conciliated.

Other dogs arrive in crates. Some fawn. One that will neither obey nor conciliate is killed. Buck watches, learning.

EXT. SEATTLE DOCK / DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

A little weazened man, PERRAULT, spatters broken English.

(SOUND: harbor creak, gulls, water slap on hull)

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that. Seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

Money passes. Buck and CURLY, a good-natured Newfoundland, are led aboard. Below, FRANCOIS—French-Canadian half-breed, swarthy giant—takes charge. SPITZ, big snow-white, steals Buck's food; Francois's whip sings first. DAVE, gloomy, wants only to be left alone.

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

Propeller still. Excitement. Francois leashes the dogs on deck. Buck's feet sink into white mush. He snorts, sniffs, licks. It bites like fire and is gone.

(SOUND: wind, soft snow hiss, men's uproarious laughter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Chaos of men, dogs, outfits. Primordial. No peace, no rest.

(SOUND: dogs snarling, sled runners, axe on wood, wind off the flats)

Curly makes friendly advances to a husky the size of a wolf. A leap, metallic clip of teeth—her face ripped open. Thirty huskies circle silent, licking chops. She goes down. They bury her. Francois swings an axe; men with clubs scatter them. Curly lies limp in bloody snow.

Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue, laughing. Buck's eyes fix on him with deathless hate.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you.

EXT. TRAIL FRINGE / CAMP - DAY

Francois harnesses Buck. Dignity hurt, but he pulls. Dave nips his hindquarters. Spitz jerks the traces. Buck learns: ho, mush, swing wide.

(SOUND: harness jingle, whip crack, sled runners on snow)

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

New dogs: BILLEE (good-natured), JOE (sour, malignant eye), SOL-LEKS (one-eyed, the Angry One). Spitz thrashes Billee; Joe faces him down. Sol-leks slashes Buck's shoulder for approaching the blind side.

EXT. SNOW CAMP - NIGHT

Buck driven from the tent with curses and utensils. Freezing. He finds Billee curled under the snow. Digs his own hole. Sleeps.

(SOUND: wind moan, distant dog howls, snow soft under paws)

Morning: buried. Fear of the trap. He bounds up in a flashing cloud of snow.

FRANCOIS
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. CHILCOOT PASS AND FROZEN LAKES - DAY

Dark break of camp. Traces snap tight. Perrault packs snow ahead of the runners. Thin ice groans under the sled. The climb toward Chilcoot; then open lakes. Hunger rides with them. Buck steals bacon—cunning now. Instincts wake. At night he points his nose at a star and howls, long and wolflike.

(SOUND: whip, panting, ice groan, one long wolf howl under aurora)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The domesticated generations fell from him. When he howled, it was his ancestors, dead and dust, howling down through the centuries.

EXT. SHORE OF LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Bleak camp on ice. Spitz steals Buck's nest. Buck springs. They circle—then starving village huskies raid. Pandemonium. Clubs. Blood. Spitz attacks Buck treacherously in the mess. Team flees onto the lake.

(SOUND: snarls, clubs on ribs, grub-box smash, wind knife-sharp)

Dawn: wounded dogs. Half the grub gone.

FRANCOIS
Ah, my frien's—mebbe it mek you mad dog, dose many bites.

EXT. THIRTY MILE RIVER AND TRAIL - DAY

Exhausting ice. Perrault breaks through, pole-saves himself. Sled breaks through with Dave and Buck—fire and run to thaw. Dolly goes mad; Francois's axe ends her. Spitz tears Buck in the aftermath; Francois whips Spitz raw.

(SOUND: ice crack, water rush, axe blow, whip)

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. Some dam fine day heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK / MOONLIT SNOW - NIGHT

Snowshoe rabbit. Pack in full cry—police dogs join. Buck leads, blood-lust pure. Spitz cuts across, kills the rabbit. Buck drives into him. Circle of sixty waits.

(SOUND: pack cry, rabbit shriek, then sudden ghostly quiet—only breaths)

They circle. Fang clashes fang. Buck rushes low—crunch—Spitz's left foreleg breaks. Then the right. Spitz staggers on three legs. The circle tightens.

(MUSIC: low tense drone building)

(SOUND: bone crunch, snow under paws, collective breath)

Buck springs. Shoulder meets shoulder. The dark circle becomes a dot on moon-flooded snow as Spitz disappears. Buck stands over the kill.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. CAMP - DAWN

Spitz gone. Buck covered in wounds. Francois points them out by firelight.

(SOUND: fire spit, morning cold creak)

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? Dat Buck two devils.

Francois tries Sol-leks at lead. Buck drives Sol-leks back. Club comes out. Buck circles beyond range—wants leadership, not escape. An hour of dodging. At last Francois throws down the club. Buck swings into the lead position, laughing as dogs laugh.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar!

Record run south. Then official orders. Francois weeps, arms around Buck. Perrault and Francois pass out of his life. A SCOTCH HALF-BREED takes the mail team—heavy toil.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL - DAY

Dave fails inside. Taken from traces, he bites back in, heart-proud. At last too weak. Train halts. Scotch half-breed walks back into timber.

(SOUND: revolver shot, distant; then bells, whips, sleds moving on)

Buck knows. Every dog knows.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Worn-out team sold. HAL—callow, cartridge belt—and CHARLES—watery eyes, fierce mustache—with MERCEDES, Charles's wife. Slipshod camp. Overloaded sled. Outside dogs added. Incompetence. Whips. Starvation.

(SOUND: whip whistle, Mercedes's weeping, sled lashings creak)

Onlookers warn. Hal will not hear. Dogs drop. Dub shot. Billee axed in the traces. Spring beauty ignored—skeletons stagger.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - MOUTH OF WHITE RIVER - DAY

They stagger in. JOHN THORNTON whittles an axe-handle. Hal whips the downed team. Buck will not rise. Club falls. Thornton springs—hurls Hal back.

(SOUND: club on flesh, Thornton's inarticulate cry, Mercedes scream)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton knocks the knife away with the axe-handle, cuts Buck's traces. Hal's team limps onto rotten ice. A section gives way—dogs and humans vanish into a yawning hole.

(SOUND: ice roar, scream cut off, water swallow)

Thornton kneels. Searches Buck for breaks. Only bruises and starvation.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. RIVER BANK CAMP - DAY

Weeks. Buck heals. SKEET, little Irish setter, washes his wounds. NIG, huge black good-natured hound, romps. Thornton takes Buck's head between rough hands, shakes him, murmurs loving oaths.

(SOUND: river run, birds, Skeet's soft whine, Thornton's low laugh)

JOHN THORNTON
God! you can all but speak!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. John Thornton had saved his life—and he was the ideal master.

Buck follows at heels, fears Thornton will vanish like the others. Yet deep in the forest a call sounds. He plunges toward it—then love draws him back to the fire.

EXT. CLIFF CREST - DAY

Thornton, HANS, PETE. Whim. Thornton sweeps an arm over the three-hundred-foot drop.

JOHN THORNTON
Jump, Buck!

Buck leaps. Men grapple him back from the edge.

(SOUND: wind off the chasm, boots scrape rock, sharp breath)

PETE
It's uncanny.

JOHN THORNTON
No—it is splendid, and it is terrible, too.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Boat flips. Thornton swept toward teeth of rock. Buck swims, feels the hand on his tail, is ordered ashore, returns with rope, strikes like a battering ram. Three broken ribs. They camp till he knits.

(SOUND: white water roar, rope strain, choking gasps)

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON / STREET - DAWSON - DAY

Boast and wager. MATTHEWSON's thousand against Buck starting a thousand pounds. Sixty below. Runners frozen. Odds three to one.

(SOUND: saloon babble spilling to street, boots on hard snow, crowd hush)

Thornton kneels, cheek to cheek.

JOHN THORNTON
(whispers)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck tightens, slacks. Gee. Haw. Mush—pistol-crack command. Muscles knot under silk fur. Sled breaks out, lurches, steadies, runs the hundred yards. Roar of men. Thornton curses him lovingly, tears streaming.

JOHN THORNTON
Sir—you can go to hell, sir.

EXT. UNCHARTED EAST - WILDERNESS - DAY

Lost-mine quest. Months. Gold like yellow butter in the pan. Moose-hide sacks piled. Buck muses by the fire—vision of the short-legged hairy man, fear, stick with stone. The call grows.

(SOUND: pan gravel, fire, distant wolf note)

One night: a long-drawn howl. Buck finds a lean timber wolf. Friendly menace. They run side by side over the divide. Buck remembers Thornton—turns back. The wild brother howls mournfully behind.

EXT. SPRUCE-BOUGH CAMP - DAY

Buck returns from a long kill—great bull moose pulled down after days—to a pregnant silence. Nig dead with an arrow. A sled-dog thrashing. Hans feathered like a porcupine. YEEHAT warriors dance in wreckage.

(SOUND: chant rising, then Buck's roar)

Buck becomes hurricane. Throat of the chief. Another. Arrows strike their own. Panic. He drags them down like deer. Returns. Pete dead in blankets. Skeet at the pool's edge. Thornton's trace leads into muddy water—and does not lead away.

(SOUND: water drip, flies, wind empty through ruined lodge)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death he knew. John Thornton was dead. It left a great void which food could not fill.

Night. Full moon. From far away, sharp yelps—the pack. Buck waits in the clearing. Boldest leaps; neck breaks. Three more bloodied back. Pack surges; he bays in a gravel angle till they draw off.

(SOUND: yelps closer, snarl, bodies in snow, then lull)

The long lean wild brother advances, whining. They touch noses. An old battle-scarred wolf howls. The pack howls. Buck howls. He runs with them, side by side with the wild brother.

EXT. MOONLIT VALLEY / OPEN SPACE AMONG TREES - NIGHT

Years thin away. A great gloriously coated wolf—splash of white down the chest, stray brown on muzzle—stands where yellow dust once spilled from rotted moose-hide sacks. He howls once, long and mournfully.

(SOUND: one mournful howl; then wind; then the many-noted song of the pack)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the long winter nights come on, he may be seen running at the head of the pack through the pale moonlight, leaping gigantic above his fellows, his great throat a-bellow as he sings a song of the younger world—which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
